      "abstract": "This repository contains the input data for the paper \u0027Modelling human activities in a system of cities\u0027. \n\nThe input data is used to run the DAVE model for Cantons Vaud and Geneva in Switzerland at 500m grid. DAVE (**D**ynamic **A**nthropogenic acti**V**ities and feedback to **E**missions) is a multi-scale agent-based modelling system. It uses a bottom-up approach to model the interactions and feedback between urban form (e.g., building morphology, materials), urban function determined by human activities and the urban micro-climate. This dataset is organized following the structure required by DAVE. Further details can be found at: \n\n- DAVE Documentation and Manual:  [10.5281/zenodo.11369892](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11369893)\n\nThe top folders containing the files for each category: Citizens, EnvironmentalVariables, InputOptions, Spatial, Time_settings, Transport, Travel_dict.\n\n**Citizens:** each citizen in each grid with household and building information\n\n**EnvironmentalVariables:** weather forcing\n\n**InputOptions:** microenvironments and age type definitions\n\n\u003cb\u003eSpatial: \u003c/b\u003e\n\n- Information on the spatial grids such as the grid list, employment rate, spatial attractor, commuting attractor. \n- Information on building such as the initial occupancy of each building (London_stebbs_building_typologies.nml actually contains building in the Swiss domain) and general thermal properties (London_stebbs_general_params.nml, currently uses London parameters but can be updated in the future) \n\n**Time_settings:** day types (weekday, holidays, holidays with school) of each day of year\n\n**Transport:** transport capacity of each mode. The probability of using each transport mode to commute at each grid. \n\n**Travel_dict:** the shortest path for each transport mode between each 500m grid in the study domain. The files are produced by MATSDA (**M**ovement **A**nd **T**ransport **S**imulations using **D**ijkstra’s **A**lgorithm) v1.0, which uses grid-based transport database and Dijkstra’s Algorithm to solve the shortest paths between each grid. Further details can be found at:\n\n- Connecting Physical and Socio-Economic Spaces for Multi-Scale Urban Modelling: A Dataset for London. Geoscience Data Journal 12: e289. https://doi.org/10.1002/gdj3.289 \n- The transport network input to MATSDA can be downloaded from SwissTopo: https://www.swisstopo.admin.ch/en/landscape-model-swisstlm3d\n- Note: a newer version of MATSDA has become available recently (not used in this study): MATSDA-roads (Movement And Transport Simulations using Dijkstra\u0027s Algorithm - roads), https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17736682\n\nTo run DAVE simulations, a file on population activity schedule/habits is also needed. We processed this file from the TimeUse+ survey conducted by ETHZ but is not able to share it due to privacy. The data from TimeUse+ survey can be requested from its authors. Further details can be found at: \n\n-  TimeUse+ main study Anonymized data, http://hdl.handle.net/20.500.11850/639241\n\n\nMore details on the processes to produce these files can be found at: \n\n- Connecting Physical and Socio-Economic Spaces for Multi-Scale Urban Modelling: A Dataset for London. Geoscience Data Journal 12: e289. https://doi.org/10.1002/gdj3.289",
      "citation-key": "item_31ea9461f47e4ac181ec2f7d804768ad",
